```ts
import type { ArcRotateCamera } from "@babylonjs/lite";

// Small enough not to be visible, large enough to count as a real change.
const NUDGE = 1e-9;

export function focusOn(camera: ArcRotateCamera, point: { x: number; y: number; z: number }): void {
  // Move the orbit centre in place. alpha, beta and radius are untouched, so the
  // eye moves by the same offset as the centre does.
  const target = camera.target;
  target.x = point.x;
  target.y = point.y;
  target.z = point.z;

  // Writing target does not mark the camera dirty. The next alpha/beta/radius
  // write that changes its value does. So write alpha to a different value and
  // back, in the same synchronous block. Both writes change the value, which
  // marks the camera dirty, and the final alpha is exactly the original.
  const alpha = camera.alpha;
  const upper = camera.upperAlphaLimit;
  const delta = upper !== undefined && alpha + NUDGE > upper ? -NUDGE : NUDGE;
  camera.alpha = alpha + delta;
  camera.alpha = alpha;
}
```

I wrote this from the type declarations and the notes alone, and I have not compiled or run it. The notes say that writing `target` in place does not mark the camera dirty (note 3), and I rely on that. The two-write alpha trick assumes the library marks dirty on each changing write rather than comparing the final value with the last rendered one; I could not confirm which. If it is the latter, the view will not update until the next frame, and the fix is to use a real 1e-9 offset and accept that alpha is off by that amount.

Other things to check: an `interpolateArcRotateCamera` run in progress will reject its promise when the target changes (note 6), and inertial offsets from a drag still in progress will keep moving the camera after the call. I also assumed `getCameraPosition` (via `worldMatrix`) refreshes through the same dirty path as the view matrix.
